/* ===========================================================================
   raid-shoutout.ttv — shout out raiders, and let mods re-shout them later.

   Twitch tells you about a raid once and then forgets it. The auto-shoutout
   usually lands in the middle of 200 people typing "RAID", so it gets missed,
   and then a mod wants to post it again by hand a minute later — by which
   point nobody can remember who it was. So: shout them out automatically,
   AND remember them so `!so` with no name works.

   Also posts a wrap-up nag on a timer, because I always overrun.

   Permissions: +read:datetime   (the nag prints the wall-clock time)
   Verbs used:  POST
   Scope:       default (`+scope:global`) — the raid handler and the command
                handler are siblings, and the second has to see what the
                first remembered.
   =========================================================================== */

// A raid landed. Twitch hands us the raider's login, how many people they
// brought, and whatever they were last streaming.
await (.raider is SOMETHING)

    /* Stream titles are free text and regularly arrive with hard line breaks
       and runs of padding in them — people put ASCII art in there. Chat takes
       exactly one line, so flatten the whole thing: `%s` is the whitespace
       class, so this replaces every run of whitespace with a single space.
       `%` also trims, so the leading/trailing junk goes too. */
    .raider_title %s ' ' -> raid_title

    // Remembered for the `!so` handler below. It's a sibling of this block,
    // and under the default scope mode siblings share bindings — which is the
    // entire reason `!so` works with no argument.
    .raider -> last_raider

    // Rotated so it doesn't read like a bot. All three mention the raider and
    // what they were doing, because that's the part people act on.
    POST any from (
        `RAID!! go follow @${ last_raider } — they were doing ${ raid_title } 💜`
        `${ .raid_size } absolute gremlins incoming from @${ last_raider } (${ raid_title })`
        `thank you @${ last_raider }!! they were streaming ${ raid_title }, go say hi`
    )

    // Big raids get a second line so the new people feel welcomed, not just
    // counted. A handful of friends dropping by doesn't need the fanfare.
    if .raid_size is or above 50
        POST * from (
            `welcome in, all ${ .raid_size } of you — grab a seat, get comfy 💜`
            `which one of you ${ .raid_size } gremlins has The Talking Stick?`
        )

// Chat commands. Fires on every message that carries a command.
await (.command is SOMETHING)

    // Mods only. Inside an `await` the subject is the chat message, and a
    // message carries the sender's badges, so this checks the sender rather
    // than the channel. It's a gate: the message stays the subject inside, so
    // `.command` and `.argument` still read off it. A non-mod runs nothing.
    using [moderator]

        when .command is

            "so":
                // `!so somebody` — an explicit name wins. SOMETHING rather than
                // `*`: Twitch sends a bare `!so` with an empty argument, and an
                // empty string is still ANYTHING.
                if .argument is SOMETHING
                    POST `everyone go follow @${ .argument } 💜`

                // Falls back to whoever raided us last.
                else if last_raider is SOMETHING
                    POST `go follow @${ last_raider }, they raided us earlier 💜`

                else
                    POST `nobody has raided yet — give me a name: !so <channel>`

            "socials":
                POST `twitch.tv/${ #name } · everything else: linktr.ee/${ #name }`

            // Anything that isn't one of ours.
            *:
                POST `never heard of !${ .command }. try !so or !socials`

/* The wrap-up nag. Its own `using` purely so the datetime grant is scoped to
   the thing that needs it — the raid handling above has no business reading
   the clock. No subject: the grant is all this header is for. */
using +read:datetime

    await 30:00
        POST `it is ${ &datetime.time() } and i said i would end at nine. send help`
